---
// Gocar — Overview. The section's landing: what the tool is and why it exists,
// a quick start, what a managed project looks like, what its dataset is, the
// boundaries it keeps, and the current release.
//
// The release facts and "what a managed project looks like" live here rather
// than on Install, so each fact has one home; Install is the command itself.
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import GocarNav from "../../components/GocarNav.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../components/PageHeader.astro";
import { GOCAR_CRATE, GOCAR_RELEASE_FACTS, mapDoc } from "../../data/gocar";
---

<Layout
    siteNav={false}
    title="Gocar — gpui-archipelago"
    description="Gocar is a Cargo subcommand for binding GPUI forks: it records which fork and release a project binds, resolves it, and verifies the result. The fork map is a view of what it measures."
>
    <GocarNav />

    <PageHeader title="Gocar" meta={GOCAR_CRATE.description} />

    <section class="section border-b border-border-divider last:border-b-0">
        <div class="content">
            <h2 class="mb-8 text-h1 font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] leading-tight">
                What it is
            </h2>
            <div class="prose">
                <p>
                    Gocar is a <code>cargo</code> subcommand. You tell it which
                    fork and release your project binds, and from then on that
                    binding is recorded, resolved, and verified by the tool
                    rather than by a <code>package = "…"</code> rename someone
                    has to get right by hand.
                </p>
                <p>
                    That matters because the forks publish the same project
                    under six package names, with unsynchronised versions. A
                    hand-edited binding that is subtly wrong usually surfaces as
                    a compile error deep in a multi-minute build — the worst
                    place to find out.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section border-b border-border-divider last:border-b-0">
        <div class="content">
            <h2 class="mb-8 text-h1 font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] leading-tight">
                Quick start
            </h2>
            <pre class="command-block"><code>{`cargo install cargo-gocar
cargo gocar new my-app --provider gpui-unofficial
cd my-app
cargo build`}</code></pre>
            <div class="prose">
                <p>
                    Gocar needs a working Rust toolchain and lands on your
                    <code>PATH</code> as <code>cargo gocar</code>; every command
                    is run from the project it manages. It writes the
                    era-correct binding unit — the engine plus the platform
                    companion that fork is version-locked to — so the scaffold
                    compiles as written.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section border-b border-border-divider last:border-b-0">
        <div class="content">
            <h2 class="mb-8 text-h1 font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] leading-tight">
                What a managed project looks like
            </h2>
            <div class="prose">
                <p>
                    A Gocar-managed project is an ordinary Cargo project: no
                    proprietary lockfile, no custom build system, no runtime
                    dependency.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        The fork binding is an ordinary Cargo package rename in
                        <code>Cargo.toml</code>, and Gocar keeps it resolved and
                        verified.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        The platform companion is locked to the exact version
                        that fork needs.
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    You can read the two files before installing anything: the
                    <a href="/map/#/configure">Fork Map's Configure view</a>
                    writes the same <code>Cargo.toml</code> and
                    <code>main.rs</code> for any fork and release it has
                    measured.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section border-b border-border-divider last:border-b-0">
        <div class="content">
            <h2 class="mb-8 text-h1 font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] leading-tight">
                The Fork Map dataset
            </h2>
            <div class="prose">
                <p>
                    <code>cargo gocar export-fork-map</code> writes the dataset
                    the <a href="/map/">Fork Map</a> renders (the
                    <code>gocar.forkmap.manifest.v1</code> bundle): the measured
                    items and their digests, the item-set fingerprints that
                    group releases into a generation, and the evidence each
                    claim traces to. Every claim links the study it came from,
                    and those studies are readable in the map itself.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        Compile badges are compile-backed: each one runs on real
                        crates.io artifacts with real <code>rustc</code>
                        versions, never on fixtures or uncompiled stubs.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        A generation is a measured fingerprint over item sets,
                        not a compiler pass — where a compile has been run, the
                        compile is the stronger evidence.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section border-b border-border-divider last:border-b-0">
        <div class="content">
            <h2 class="mb-8 text-h1 font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] leading-tight">
                What it does not do
            </h2>
            <div class="prose">
                <p>
                    V1 does not rewrite your code. Where a change is needed it
                    names the site and the reason, and leaves the edit to you:
                    the study that ran the whole flow on a real app found the
                    auto-driven share of a real break is zero, and left the move
                    manual on purpose — <a href={mapDoc("9")}>doc 09</a>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    It also refuses what it cannot vouch for: a prerelease floor
                    (<a href={mapDoc("7")}>doc 07</a>), and a second fork
                    anywhere in the dependency graph — including one a UI kit
                    pulls in transitively (<a href={mapDoc("10")}>doc 10</a>).
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section border-b border-border-divider last:border-b-0">
        <div class="content">
            <h2 class="mb-8 text-h1 font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] leading-tight">
                Current release
            </h2>
            <div class="table-scroll">
                <table class="min-w-96 max-sm:min-w-0">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col">Fact</th>
                            <th scope="col">Value</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        {GOCAR_RELEASE_FACTS.map((fact) => (
                            <tr>
                                <td data-label="Fact">{fact.label}</td>
                                <td
                                    class="font-mono text-code"
                                    data-label="Value"
                                >
                                    {fact.href ? (
                                        <a href={fact.href}>{fact.value}</a>
                                    ) : (
                                        fact.value
                                    )}
                                </td>
                            </tr>
                        ))}
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p class="table-note">
                The version on this page is maintained by hand, so it can lag
                the registry. <a href={GOCAR_CRATE.cratesUrl}>crates.io</a> is
                the authority.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</Layout>
